{"kind":"request","text":"RV-1 source-name question before production code. This records the earlier workroom-chat question as a durable request under RV-1, not a new implementation scope.\n\nRV-1 c6276dec directs Replay to prove a used foreign genesis from its canonical bytes and recorded kind, without a caller anchor. admitSequenceGenesis authenticates the scope address, genesis hash, seed and kind. It does not authenticate the declaration name recorded in retained_input.under. A digest-pinned declaration's name is not present in the genesis bytes; its digest is.\n\nCurrent behavior at main 4cef11d2: Replay.#privateCopy (packages/replay/src/verify.ts:1658-1679) requires an exact caller anchor before it accepts either retained-name metadata or an archive's finite source alias. TRUSTS.anchors explicitly includes each retained definition name. Source-name checks use that alias to judge fact fields and deliveries. A self-proved genesis must not silently convert mutable alias metadata into trusted source identity, and the fact proof must not create Native factory authority.\n\nPlease rule on the name boundary. Proposed behavior: use checked genesis bytes for the fact proof; authenticate a native name against its pinned version and the existing source-identity policy; authenticate a declared name from canonical declaration bytes under the digest that the genesis pins, using the receiving scope's retained declaration when supplied. Keep the existing reserved-name and native-origin checks. If the required name bytes or identity proof are absent, answer incomplete rather than invent the source name. An exact caller-anchor path may retain its existing explicit name trust, but must remain visibly anchored if that trust is needed.\n\nAlternative, if RV-1 is meant to prove the fact while keeping its source name on trust: state exactly which existing report trust expresses that metadata assumption without claiming source replay or Native admission. The report currently renders dependencies.verified as shown by replay of the source scope; a self-proved genesis also needs accurate rendering without adding a kernel record form.\n\nThe concrete case is an ordinary declared source whose genesis is held as a foreign use, with its source journal private. Genesis bytes pin its declaration digest, and the copied under name could be changed independently. The three owning witness drafts are preserved on builder/rv1-used-genesis-20261113: a private genesis proved without history or anchor (its fixture supplies the pinned declaration bytes), a damaged copy refused, and a later entry that still needs its exact anchor. No production workaround, test, compiler or build has run. Production code waits for this ruling.","body":{"conditions":"One ruling states how RV-1 authenticates or explicitly trusts a retained source name when the fact is a self-proving genesis; it states the missing-name outcome and preserves later-entry anchors and native-origin checks.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}